WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:13.530
[Music]  [Audio Description - not captioned]

00:00:13.598 --> 00:00:19.310
[Corey Lester, PhD] My proposal to the National&nbsp;
Library of Medicine was to develop and design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:25.380
artificial intelligence. Specifically, a&nbsp;
computer vision model which could look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:32.795
a picture of pills inside a medication bottle and&nbsp;
accurately identify the contents of that bottle.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:43.158
We are testing how that model can&nbsp;
be used in collaboration with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.158 --> 00:00:45.902
human so that errors don't happen in the pharmacy.

00:00:48.167 --> 00:00:51.325
[Raed Al Kontar, PhD] How do you&nbsp;
teach a machine - if you want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.325 --> 00:00:55.800
a machine to differentiate between an&nbsp;
anomaly in medical dispensing or not?

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.220
What you do is - you give the&nbsp;
machine images of anomalies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:04.703
you give the machine images of good medications,&nbsp;
and you tell them - this is good, this is bad.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:35.100
When the algorithm can make this link, then if&nbsp;
you give the algorithm a new image, the algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:39.420
can predict - this image belongs to which&nbsp;
class? Is it the good group or the bad group.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:45.660
So fundamentally, it's a matching&nbsp;
decision done in a fancy mathematical way.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:50.050
[Lester] So the way we see this being implemented&nbsp;
is - once we have that picture of pills in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.050 --> 00:01:55.320
bottle, we can use this model to predict&nbsp;
what medication it is in that image and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.760
cross-reference it with the expected or intended&nbsp;
medication that was supposed to be filled.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:05.011
In this way, we can support pharmacists&nbsp;
in their double-check verification of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.011 --> 00:02:07.438
patient getting the right&nbsp;
pill in the right bottle.

00:02:07.438 --> 00:02:13.020
[Kontar] The results were surprisingly&nbsp;
positive and we found that our algorithm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:17.128
can predict with an accuracy above 99.5 percent.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:30.292
But what one thing that we are afraid of - even&nbsp;
those 0.5 percent of errors can be critical.

00:02:30.292 --> 00:02:36.132
Our hope is that, in those cases where the&nbsp;
algorithm is wrong, the algorithm can tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.132 --> 00:02:41.394
us that it is not confident in its results.&nbsp;
Then at least, the human can double-check.

00:02:45.751 --> 00:02:51.042
[Xi Jessie Yang, PhD] Our next step is to conduct&nbsp;
the experiment with pharmacists and through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.042 --> 00:02:57.360
that we are going to track how their trust&nbsp;
change over time on a trial-to-trial basis.

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.460
So its through the experiment we&nbsp;
can collect participants responses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:07.140
their physiological signals,&nbsp;
and we want to use eye tracking.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:13.440
And by analyzing their responses and signals,&nbsp;
we can track how their trust changes over time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.131
how their behaviors, their&nbsp;
performance changes over time.

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:22.672
[Lester] The ultimate goal of this project&nbsp;
is to develop safer systems that can be used&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.672 --> 00:03:27.157
to save lives and prevent medication&nbsp;
errors from causing harm to patients.

00:03:27.157 --> 00:03:32.125
[Yang] It means tons to me. As an engineer,&nbsp;
I believe our ultimate mission is to propose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.125 --> 00:03:37.550
solutions that can be used to enhance people's&nbsp;
life. Being part of the projects doing just that.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:40.055
[Lester] The National Library&nbsp;
of Medicine's financial support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.055 --> 00:03:42.600
of this project has really been invaluable.

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:47.820
So really, they've been instrumental in&nbsp;
making all of this research possible.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:52.997
[Kontar] Without it, we would not have had&nbsp;
the capability to collect all this data,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.997 --> 00:03:57.120
to do all this analysis, and generate&nbsp;
the promising results we have right now.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.245
[Music continues]

00:04:01.421 --> 00:04:03.255
[Music fades to silence]

00:04:03.268 --> 00:04:06.177
[Silence]

